{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789966891,"nonce":"xCQEwNBv4OzBK5XJJH298ZT1c0U=","title":"tiered: a function optimised alone sees its callees unoptimised, so a constructor stays a call","message":"When the tiered runtime optimises one function in a scratch module (`run_interp_safe_opts_cached` on first call), the inliner's `Callees` map holds the other functions as lowered, not as they are after their own optimisation. A Python constructor `Node$new` as lowered is `malloc` + field defaults + `call Node$__init__`; after its own optimisation it is a 16-instruction leaf. `build` in bench_binary_trees.py is optimised with the lowered body in view, so `Node$new` classifies as call-bearing and every one of its four sites in `build` (no loop: `build` is recursive) is refused with \"callee with calls outside a loop\". The fixed point in `run_module_with` would have caught it (round 1 inlines `__init__` into `Node$new`, round 2 inlines `Node$new` into `build`), but only when both are in `ids_to_optimize` at once.\n\nSeen with `ZYNTAX_TRACE_INLINE=1 ZYPY_LLVM=1 zypy run crates/zyntax_python/benchmarks/bench_binary_trees.py` (the `refuses` lines); the tier1 HIR of `build` still has `call @Node$new`.\n\nOptions: optimise a function's small direct callees before it (a callee that classifies as a leaf once its own leaves are folded in); or let the classifier see through one level, counting a call as real only when its target is not itself an inlinable leaf without real calls. The second bounds growth to one level of leaves and needs no pipeline change.","files":null}]}